3 TEXT &amp;lt;p&amp;gt;Norwich Bulletin&amp;lt;br /&amp;gt;Publication place: Norwich, New London, Connecticut, USA&amp;lt;br /&amp;gt;Date: Sep 18 1918&amp;lt;br /&amp;gt;Text: "...AUTOMOBILE UPSET ON NORWICH TOWN ROAD An automobile owned by Simon Els
4 CONC ter of Colchestet turned turtle while en route from Colchester to New London near Norwich Town at 5.30 o'clock Tuesday morning and the five men in the machine were shaken up bruised, but all escaped serious injury. and Elster was the driver of th
4 CONC e machine and the four passengers, employes of the Groton Iron Works, gave the names of Nathan Litinsky, Kalmanovitch, Kalorovitch and Volak. 11tinsky incurred a sprain of the left wrist and a slight eut over the eye, but the other men were abl
4 CONC e to report for work. ..."&amp;lt;br /&amp;gt;About this sourceIn 1910, the Norwich Bulletin advertised that it had the largest circulation of any paper in eastern Connecticut and that its readers could be found in &ldquo;forty-nine towns, one hu
4 CONC ndred and sixty-five postal districts, and forty-one Rural Free Delivery District routes.&rdquo; A subscription base of around 7,000, the editors claimed, meant that the Bulletin had nearly 40,000 readers a day. A Republican newspaper launched i
4 CONC n 1895 during the Progressive Era, the Bulletin overtly courted its two most natural constituencies: the small towns, villages, and rural areas of the eastern part of Connecticut and adjacent Rhode Island, and the banking, industrial, and commerc
4 CONC ial movers and shakers based in the town of Norwich itself.The editor of the Norwich Bulletin summarized well the paper&rsquo;s dual missions. First, the Bulletin would &ldquo;spare no expense&rdquo; in the effort to &ldquo;cover its field&rdquo
4 CONC ; by maintaining 80 regular correspondents in the small centers of the eastern part of Connecticut. Second, the paper would &ldquo;build and boom&rdquo; the banking, commercial and industrial center of Norwich. Correspondents from the small town
4 CONC s sent in short reports on local events, social activities, and family news. The feature writer of &ldquo;A Farmer Speaks to Farmers&rdquo; commiserated with the beleaguered agriculturalists of the rural areas. At the same time, the Bulletin appe
4 CONC ars to have been the trusted aggregator of annual reports from the financial institutions, cotton and woolen mills, builders, and arms manufacturers whose owners formed the economic elite of Norwich and nurtured many of the important Republican p
4 CONC oliticians who operated in state and local government. Each January 1st issue also carried large photo spreads of industrial, institutional, and commercial buildings completed within the preceding calendar year. As Norwich was historically a cent
4 CONC er for New England trade, the Bulletin, in addition, published Atlantic maritime news. The Bulletin&rsquo;s full Associated Press service was mentioned in the publisher&rsquo;s statement in 1910 as a source of pride.The masthead featured a logo t
4 CONC hat traced the paper&rsquo;s lineage to 1791, when Ebenezer Bushnell founded the Weekly Register (1791-1795). A new owner, Thomas Hubbard, changed the name to the Chelsea Courier (1796-1798), and then over subsequent decades and changes of owners
4 CONC hip, it appeared as the Courier (1798-1809), the Norwich Courier (1809-1845), the Norwich Weekly Courier (1845-1859), the Norwich Weekly Courier (1860-?), the Norwich Courier (?-1927), and other titles. In 1858, the Courier was purchased by a pri
4 CONC nting firm called Manning, Perry, and Company and these owners attempted to publish an evening paper, the Norwich Daily Courier (1858-1859). Soon Homer Bliss and Issac Bromley joined Manning&rsquo;s firm. They decided that a morning paper would b
4 CONC e more successful than an evening daily, and the Norwich Morning Bulletin (1858-1895) was born.In 1863, a group of prominent businessmen said to be led by the cotton manufacturer John F. Slater and the banker Lorenzo Blackstone formed the joint s
4 CONC tock company known as the Bulletin Company. In 1895, the company renamed the morning paper and launched it as the Norwich Bulletin (1895-2011). Charles D. Noyes and William H. Oat purchased the Bulletin Company in 1898. Between the company&rsquo;
4 CONC s founding and 1922, many important businessmen and Republican Party leaders were associated with it. These include Noyes, himself a leader of the state party organization, and Henry H. Gallup, a banker and manufacturer who served as Connecticu
4 CONC t State Treasurer. The Norwich Bulletin was published until 2011 and a successor newspaper, the Bulletin, continues to this day&amp;lt;/p&amp;gt;
